for climate campaigners BP is a pariah  it's art sponsorships make institutions  like the British Museum a target for  protesters now it's set landmark targets  of its own committing BP to be carbon  neutral for all the oil and gas it  produces and to cut the carbon intensity  of half of everything it sells by 2050  it's a striking first step for their new  chief executive Bernard Looney a  lifelong oil man promising to transform  the energy giant just two weeks into the  job I get it  the world does have a carbon budget it  is finite and it is running out fast and  we need a rapid transition to Net Zero  BP is prized by investors for the  dividends it pays more than 8 billion  pounds last year but mr. Looney says  this will not be a choice between the  planet and our pensions you talk about  the priorities of the people you speak  to but personally which number matters  more to you hitting these carbon targets  or the dividend it's a great question to  ask it's also an easy question to answer  they're all important and in many ways  they serve each other we have to be  financially strong because that is how  we will make that transition climate  activists and campaigners welcome BP's  commitments but warned they may be too  little too late they're recognizing  we've got a window in which we can act  to protect and defend life on Earth  however Zero Carbon by 2050 is planning  for disaster and and it's a disaster in  which we lose everything  that would reach arish BP has spent the  last decade trying to recover its  reputation from the Deepwater Horizon  oil spill and investors say they can no  longer afford to ignore climate concerns  the top priority of our planners is that  investment is made with purpose  and that it makes a build a better world  rather than doing the exact opposite to  actually create climate change BP will  still be pumping oil and gas in 30 years  time and these targets will not address  the climate crisis alone but a company  that currently can't give its money away  in sponsorships may prove a model for  others  Paul Kelso sky news
 

